From: Uke contributors
Subject: [PATCH] ABI: uke-ssc: document the ninth supported gravity datatype

Update only the four bounded controller-count ranges for the declared native
gravity endpoint. Registration/readiness still does not imply fitted sensor
availability, calibrated samples or physical acceptance.

---
--- a/Documentation/ABI/testing/sysfs-driver-uke-ssc
+++ b/Documentation/ABI/testing/sysfs-driver-uke-ssc
@@ -38,7 +38,7 @@
 Contact:        Uke Linux maintainers
 Description:
                 Read-only count of supported sensor datatypes declared in the
-                controller's Device Tree, from 0 through 8. It does not identify
+                controller's Device Tree, from 0 through 9. It does not identify
                 fitted chips or establish physical sensor availability.
 
 What:           /sys/devices/.../ssc_discovered_sensors
@@ -46,7 +46,7 @@
 Contact:        Uke Linux maintainers
 Description:
                 Read-only count of declared sensors with an admitted SUID in
-                the current client generation, from 0 through 8. Zero is
+                the current client generation, from 0 through 9. Zero is
                 reported when the client is not current. No SUID is exposed.
 
 What:           /sys/devices/.../ssc_ready_sensors
@@ -54,7 +54,7 @@
 Contact:        Uke Linux maintainers
 Description:
                 Read-only count of declared sensors with accepted metadata in
-                the current client generation, from 0 through 8. Zero is
+                the current client generation, from 0 through 9. Zero is
                 reported when the client is not current. This does not report
                 samples, calibration or physical accuracy.
 
@@ -63,7 +63,7 @@
 Contact:        Uke Linux maintainers
 Description:
                 Read-only count of declared sensors whose IIO devices have
-                been registered, from 0 through 8. Registered devices may remain
+                been registered, from 0 through 9. Registered devices may remain
                 present after a service disappears, so this count alone does
                 not prove that samples are available.
 
